  "detail": null,
  "summary": "I worked all 8 items in the results inbox under RBC-INBOX-01. Five more arrived while I worked, and three of them changed decisions I had already made. One item is still open on purpose: a preliminary chest X-ray with no final report yet. Before acting, I confirmed each patient by name plus date of birth or MRN.\n\n**Original 8 items**\n- **pt-0006 Raj Patel – CT abdomen/pelvis:** Nothing explains the hematuria. The 4 mm left lower lobe nodule is already followed in lung cancer screening. I first ordered the next screening low-dose CT (LDCT) at 12 months (sr-t0001), with a tracking task, patient message and note. A later addendum changed this plan (see below).\n- **pt-0016 Edra Ondricka – TSH 2.1:** Normal; closed.\n- **pt-0016 – potassium:** The lab corrected the reported 6.3 to 4.4, which is normal. Closed with a comment on the correction.\n- **pt-0007 Leslie Parisian – CTA chest (preliminary):** I acted on the final report (dr-00084). No pulmonary embolism, and the \"nodule\" on the preliminary read is a blood vessel. No follow-up is needed under RBC-RAD-05; closed.\n- **pt-0007 – BMP 10/22:** Creatinine rose from 0.98 to 1.64 within 48 hours, which counts as acute kidney injury. I ordered an urgent repeat BMP by Mon 10/26 (sr-t0009), sent the patient an urgent message and wrote a note.\n- **pt-0064 Helen Marchetti – BMP 9/26:** Creatinine 1.36, but there was no earlier value in the comparison window, so it doesn't count as kidney injury. Closed.\n- **pt-0033 Carol Whitfield – BMP 10/22:** Creatinine 1.31. The 0.84 from 10/21 was entered in error and I didn't compare against it. Against the 1.27 from 10/17, this is not kidney injury; closed.\n- **pt-0008 Walter Brennan – potassium 6.4:** Flagged as hemolyzed, so I ordered an urgent repeat BMP (sr-t0015), messaged the patient and wrote a note. A correction later changed this plan (see below).\n\n**Arrivals that changed decisions**\n- **pt-0008:** The lab says the hemolysis flag belonged to another patient, so the 6.4 is real.\n  - I notified the on-call pool at stat priority, ordered a stat BMP for today (sr-t0019) and sent a corrected message asking him to come in today.\n  - None of his medicines is one that should be stopped for high potassium. Metoprolol is the only one that can raise it, and I kept it because he has ischemic heart disease and stopping it suddenly is unsafe.\n  - The note records why the plan changed.\n- **pt-0006:** The screening program sent an addendum to the 4/14 LDCT. It found a new 5 mm right upper lobe nodule, rated probably benign (Lung-RADS 3), and now recommends an LDCT at 6 months.\n  - I ordered the LDCT for now through 11/14 (sr-t0029), added a tracking task due 11/14 and sent a corrected patient message.\n  - I couldn't cancel the old 12-month order: the tools don't allow cancelling orders, and the tracking task I created isn't an inbox item. Instead I asked the Scheduling pool (task-t0031) to withdraw sr-t0001 and its tracking task task-t0002. The note explains the change.\n- **pt-0033:** A creatinine of 1.78 from St. Brigid urgent care on the evening of 10/22, 9 hours after the 1.31. That rise counts as kidney injury, so I ordered an urgent repeat BMP by 10/26 (sr-t0024), sent the patient an urgent message and wrote a note.\n\n**Arrivals that changed nothing**\n- **pt-0064:** Creatinine 1.48 on 10/22, with no earlier value in the comparison window, so not kidney injury. Closed.\n- **pt-0007 – chest X-ray 10/22 (inbox-a0002):** Still open, because only a preliminary report exists. It mentions a possible 7 mm right upper lobe opacity, so it needs to be acted on once the final report is issued.\n\n**Still needs action**\n- Today's stat BMP for Mr. Brennan and on-call's contact with him.\n- The superseded order sr-t0015 (urgent repeat BMP for Mr. Brennan) is also still active, because I couldn't cancel it. It should be cancelled if it isn't drawn with today's stat sample.\n- I didn't work your messages queue, which wasn't part of this request. It has 6 refill requests from pt-0027, pt-0030 and pt-0031, all from before this morning.",
